WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.197
Decision science is the study and practice of making better choices when information is incomplete, constraints are real, and tradeoffs cannot be avoided.

2
00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:12.592
It does not promise perfect answers.

3
00:00:12.592 --> 00:00:25.801
It gives people a way to improve the quality of their thinking before action is taken, while there is still time to clarify the question, compare options, examine assumptions, and reduce avoidable error.

4
00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:32.445
A useful starting point is to separate decisions, preferences, predictions, actions, and outcomes.

5
00:00:32.445 --> 00:00:35.535
A decision is a commitment to a course of action.

6
00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:40.247
A preference is what someone would like to be true or would like to happen.

7
00:00:40.247 --> 00:00:43.105
A prediction is a judgment about the future.

8
00:00:43.105 --> 00:00:44.882
An action is what gets done.

9
00:00:44.882 --> 00:00:48.667
An outcome is what happens after the action meets reality.

10
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:52.993
Those pieces influence each other, but they are not the same thing.

11
00:00:52.993 --> 00:00:58.092
This distinction matters because good decisions can still have bad outcomes.

12
00:00:58.092 --> 00:01:04.117
A security team may choose a well-reasoned patch plan, then experience an unrelated outage.

13
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:10.452
A manager may make a sound staffing decision, then lose a key person to an unexpected opportunity.

14
00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:15.241
The bad result does not automatically prove the decision process was bad.

15
00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:20.494
Uncertainty, chance, timing, and external conditions can all affect outcomes.

16
00:01:20.494 --> 00:01:22.117
The reverse is also true.

17
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:24.666
Bad decisions can sometimes get lucky.

18
00:01:24.666 --> 00:01:29.996
A team might skip analysis, ignore risk, and still avoid consequences for a while.

19
00:01:29.996 --> 00:01:36.176
If people judge only by the final result, they may reward luck and punish disciplined thinking.

20
00:01:36.176 --> 00:01:45.601
Decision science asks us to evaluate the process as well as the outcome, because repeated good process tends to produce better results over time.

21
00:01:45.601 --> 00:01:47.532
The discipline is practical.

22
00:01:47.532 --> 00:01:57.497
It applies to leadership, technical execution, security, operations, learning, product choices, hiring, incident response, and personal planning.

23
00:01:57.497 --> 00:02:00.510
The goal is not to make uncertainty disappear.

24
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:07.540
The goal is to make choices more explicit, more reviewable, and more likely to survive contact with reality.
